WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.520
In Chapter 1, the GPU doubles 5 input values.

00:00:04.280 --> 00:00:05.320
Let's follow one of them.

00:00:05.760 --> 00:00:07.900
1 .0 becomes 2 .0.

00:00:08.560 --> 00:00:10.740
The values are stored in GPU memory.

00:00:11.540 --> 00:00:16.020
A streaming multiprocessor, or SM, supplies the hardware
that executes

00:00:16.020 --> 00:00:16.560
instructions.

00:00:17.160 --> 00:00:19.900
We will follow a load, a multiply, and a store.

00:00:21.415 --> 00:00:23.595
We start by inspecting thread zero.

00:00:24.255 --> 00:00:27.815
Its load instruction requests the value at input index
zero.

00:00:28.535 --> 00:00:32.995
Load and store hardware connects memory operations with
the thread's working

00:00:32.995 --> 00:00:33.515
registers.

00:00:34.335 --> 00:00:37.815
The value 1 .0 is now available in a register.

00:00:38.415 --> 00:00:40.995
Nothing has been written to the output array yet.

00:00:42.491 --> 00:00:46.851
The multiply instruction uses the loaded value and the
multiplier two.

00:00:47.551 --> 00:00:52.671
Floating point arithmetic hardware produces 2 .0 and
returns the result to

00:00:52.671 --> 00:00:53.151
a register.

00:00:53.891 --> 00:00:56.431
The arithmetic unit is hardware shared by threads.

00:00:57.111 --> 00:01:00.291
It is not a little processor permanently owned by thread
zero.

00:01:01.011 --> 00:01:04.751
The result is still inside the SM, not in the output
array.

00:01:06.243 --> 00:01:08.263
The store is a separate instruction.

00:01:09.083 --> 00:01:13.623
Load and store hardware writes 2 .0 from the thread's
result register into

00:01:13.623 --> 00:01:16.463
output index 0 in GPU memory.

00:01:17.263 --> 00:01:19.323
We have now followed the whole value path.

00:01:19.923 --> 00:01:23.443
Memory to register, arithmetic, then register to memory.

00:01:24.994 --> 00:01:28.714
Now inspect thread one, whose input is 2 .0.

00:01:29.394 --> 00:01:31.554
Its load supplies its own working register.

00:01:32.274 --> 00:01:34.194
Thread zero's values remain separate.

00:01:34.674 --> 00:01:38.874
We are changing which values we inspect, not choosing the
GPU's execution

00:01:38.874 --> 00:01:39.274
order.

00:01:39.994 --> 00:01:43.294
A GPU can execute instructions for groups of threads
together.

00:01:43.814 --> 00:01:47.454
This picture isolates two threads to make their values
easy to follow.

00:01:48.970 --> 00:01:54.090
Thread 1's multiply uses the same depicted floating point
hardware, this time

00:01:54.090 --> 00:01:55.630
producing 4 .0.

00:01:56.370 --> 00:01:59.090
Its store writes that result to output index 1.

00:01:59.790 --> 00:02:03.730
Each thread has its own working values, while execution
units are shared.

00:02:04.470 --> 00:02:09.390
The boxes show hardware roles, not how many units a real
SM contains or

00:02:09.390 --> 00:02:11.290
exactly when instructions execute.

00:02:12.883 --> 00:02:14.143
Try one more case.

00:02:14.823 --> 00:02:18.443
A third thread loads 3 .0 and multiplies it by two.

00:02:19.143 --> 00:02:20.763
Its store instruction has not run.

00:02:21.343 --> 00:02:22.743
Where is the result at this point?

00:02:23.163 --> 00:02:25.403
In a working register or in the output array?

00:02:26.003 --> 00:02:27.483
Pause here and explain why.

00:02:31.920 --> 00:02:35.380
The result, 6 .0, is in a working register.

00:02:36.100 --> 00:02:38.200
The store is what writes it to the output array.

00:02:38.860 --> 00:02:41.280
That is the distinction to take back to Chapter 1.

00:02:41.800 --> 00:02:44.700
Threads describe executions with separate working values.

00:02:45.480 --> 00:02:49.240
The SM provides shared hardware for loads, arithmetic and
stores.

00:02:50.040 --> 00:02:54.100
This is a conceptual explanation, not a trace of the
kernel PyTorch

00:02:54.100 --> 00:02:54.540
selected.
